{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "welcome.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Welcome to ChalkMath",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "ChalkMath is a notebook for mathematics that **shows its work**. Type an expression into a cell and press **Enter**: the answer comes back with every step that produced it, each named and explained the way a textbook would.\n\nEverything runs in your browser. Nothing you type is sent to a server, and your notebooks are kept in this browser until you export them.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(x^2 * sin(x), x)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "That is the product rule, then the power rule, each a step you can read. A few things to try:\n\n- **Show or hide the steps** with the `Work` button beside a cell (it appears when you hover), or View › Show all work.\n- **Click any part of an answer**, a single term or a whole fraction, and the panel below says which rule produced it and traces it back through the steps.\n- **Change a cell** and press Enter again. The cells below keep their answers until you run them.\n- Press **Enter in the empty cell at the bottom** to start your own work.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Typing math",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A cell with a fraction, a power, a root, an integral, a sum or a matrix is **typeset**: you read and edit the math as it would be written on paper. Click into one of these and change it.\n\n- `/` starts a fraction and `^` a power; **Tab** goes to the next empty slot.\n- `\\int`, `\\sum`, `\\sqrt` or `\\mat2x3`, then space, inserts one to fill in.\n- The **Text** button beside a cell (or Ctrl/⌘+Shift+M) shows the same cell as the text the engine reads; **Visual** turns it back. View › Math input sets it for every cell.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x/(x^2 + 1), x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "mode": "visual"
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(1/2^k, k, 0, 5)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "mode": "visual"
    },
    {
      "src": "Algebra",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand((x+1)^3)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "factor(x^2 + 2*x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst(x^2 + 1, x, 3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Arithmetic is exact: fractions stay fractions and roots stay roots. `N(…)` gives a decimal when you want one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sqrt(8) + sqrt(18)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(sqrt(2))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Names and functions",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`let` names a value or defines a function for the rest of the notebook. `%` is the previous answer.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let f = x^3 - 3x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(f, x, 2)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let sq(x) = x^2 + 1",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(sq(x), x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(%, x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Calculus",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(sin(x^2), x)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`integrate` finds an antiderivative and then **checks** it: it differentiates its own answer and only replies if that gives your function back. With bounds it is the definite integral.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2 + sin(x), x)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(cos(t)*sin(t), t, 0, 2pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(k^2, k, 1, 10)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Linear algebra",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Matrices are written row by row: commas between entries, semicolons between rows. `rref` records each row operation as its own step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1,2,3;4,5,6;7,8,10])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "det([a,b;c,d])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dot([1,2,3],[4,5,6])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Plots",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([sin(x), cos(x)], x, 0, 2pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([x^2, diff(x^2, x)], x, -3, 3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Complex numbers",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "ℯ^(pi*i)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(1+i)*(2-i)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "abs(3+4i)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A little further",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same engine draws Fourier series as spinning circles, reasons about finite orders, and reduces λ-terms one step at a time.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "epicycles(ℯ^(i*t) + 1/2*ℯ^(-3i*t), t)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let D = divisors(12)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(D, 4, 6)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "add 2 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A notebook can also hold **Lean cells** (Edit › Add Lean cell): Lean 4 itself, running in your browser, checks definitions and proofs as you type them, and the panel's **Lean goals** tab shows what is left to prove at the cursor. Lean is a large download the first time, so this notebook has none; the orders-and-lattices example has its proofs in them.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Where to next",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "- **Help › Documentation** explains the notebook, lookups and Lean cells, and lists every command with examples you can click to run.\n- **File › Examples** opens longer notebooks: drawing a llama with Fourier series, and a course on orders and lattices with its proofs in Lean cells.\n- **Help › Keyboard shortcuts** lists the keys. Type `\\pi`, `\\lam` or another `\\`-name for a symbol, and **Tab** completes a command.\n- **File › Save** keeps a notebook in this browser. **Export to file** and **Copy link to notebook** are for sharing.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
